-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Boundary Conditions:** The method requires 2 voxels on either side of the query point. At volume boundaries, values must be clamped, mirrored, or wrapped. Clamping is common but can introduce artifacts at edges [G].
-- **Precision:** High computational cost can lead to precision issues if not using double precision for intermediate calculations, though single precision is standard for real-time rendering [S3, unofficial].
-- **Artifacts:** While smoother, cubic interpolation can introduce ringing artifacts (overshoot/undershoot) near sharp discontinuities if the underlying data is not smooth [S4].
+**Parameters:**
+- Kernel parameters $B, C$: Define the specific cubic shape (e.g., Catmull-Rom: $B=0, C=0.5$; Mitchell-Netravali: $B=1/3, C=1/3$) [S1, unofficial].
+- Boundary handling: Clamping, wrapping, or reflection. Clamping is common to avoid artifacts at edges [G].
+
+**Edge cases and pitfalls:**
+- **Boundary conditions:** Accessing indices $i-1$ or $i+2$ may go out of bounds. Proper clamping or mirroring is required [G].
+- **Precision:** High computational cost can lead to accumulation errors in large volumes if not optimized [S3, unofficial].
+- **Artifacts:** While smoother, cubic interpolation can introduce ringing artifacts near sharp discontinuities if the kernel has negative lobes [S4].
 
